course: dictionary
lesson: 10417
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. The word trip is a good example. «Holding the handrail lets you catch yourself if you trip or slip.» Here trip means catching your foot on something and nearly falling, because the handrail stops you from falling.
explain: «One place left on the school trip.» Here trip is a journey to a place and back, often a short one. Ask yourself what the sentence is about: a place on a journey, not a foot catching on a step. Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Trip can also be a noun or a verb, and the sentence shows which one it is. To trip someone up means to make them fall, or to make them make a mistake. A round trip is going somewhere and coming back again.
fact: Trip can be a noun (a journey) or a verb (to catch your foot and fall).
fact: A trip is a journey to a place and back, often short, like a school trip.
fact: To trip is to catch your foot on something and almost fall, or fall.
fact: To trip someone up is to make them fall, or to make them make a mistake.
fact: A round trip is going and coming back.
exercise: «Holding the handrail lets you catch yourself if you trip or slip.» In this sentence, trip means | A small bag for food | *To catch your foot and nearly fall | To sing loudly | A journey somewhere and back
exercise: «One place left on the school trip.» What does trip mean here? | Tidy toys away | To fall over | *A journey to a place and back, often short | A pair of shoes
exercise: Which phrase means going somewhere and coming back? | *Round trip | Tripping | Trip up | Trips over
test: Mia says, «Sam tripped me up on the stairs.» What does tripped me up mean? | *Made me fall | Waved to me | Gave me a present | Carried me upstairs
test: «Tidy your toys away so nobody trips over them.» Which meaning fits trips over? | *Catches a foot and falls | A school form | A permission slip | A journey
exam: In «A girl lost a school trip», what does trip mean? | A feeling of sadness | A kind of bread | *A journey to a place and back | To catch your foot and fall
